					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mark-penrice" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1858819); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1858819&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608843824" alt="Mark Penrice" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1858819)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mark-penrice" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1858819); return false;" >Mark Penrice</a></span> <span class="odate time_1502336472 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Aug 2017 03:41</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3568110">
									

<p>I think the implication is that it is indeed fictional, and an anomalous meme, which has serious psychosomatic effects (including spontaneous human combustion - finally a reason for it?) arising from its cognitohazardous effect.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Including, for example, perceiving that you are entering a body of water, which will stop you from breathing, but finding to your utter terror that *it provides no bouyancy whatsoever*. Thus you cannot do anything to keep your head above it. Rather like an ocean of CO2 or similar.</p>
<p>The horizontal movement difficulties are likely more memetic - if you've ever tried to walk, rather than swim around in deepish water, particularly tidal sea water with strong waves, you'll know that it's pretty difficult even though the density is only slightly greater than 1.0&#160;g/ml, due to the drag effects and its own momentum. Like a strong wind, but without needing to move fast to cause serious difficulty. And if tides have become massively intensified, with a range of many tens of metres where previously they might have only gone up and down by 2 or 3 metres instead, that'll create one mother of a riptide. People can already easily be pulled out to sea, and sucked underwater, with just one much weaker moon moving things around.</p>
<p>(plus rivers can become impossible to wade or even drive across without being knocked over or washed away, even at points where they're only a few inches deep, when their flow rate gets much beyond walking pace&#8230; moving water is a forceful thing)</p>
<p>And then when you consider they're attempting to wade and, if the level gets higher, swim through entirely imaginary water that therefore doesn't provide any kind of significant propulsive force in response to arm strokes or leg kicks, or sculling of the surface&#8230; but are still affected by what the water itself does&#8230; that's a recipe for becoming totally caught up in the current and unable to make headway.</p>
<p>The bit about it seeming to be much denser is entirely superfluous in light of that, really. It doesn't NEED to be denser, and in fact any substance with the stated density, on earth at STP, would either be a solid, or a liquid so viscous and slow flowing that it would make the pitch drop experiment seem rapid, and therefore may as well be solid when measured on a human timescale.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p><em>For the sake and sanity of our mobile readers, please collapse long posts. ~Zyn</em></p>

